# hi's own flags, one row each:
#   <flag>|<argument>|<needs>|<script var>|<first arg>|<help>
#
# The one place they are spelled. hi.sh reads it to dispatch (a row with a
# script var execs ${!var} with the arg first; the rest are hi.sh's own case
# arms) and to print --help; common/targets.sh reads it so `hi --<TAB>` offers
# the same list. <argument> is what the flag takes on the command line, as
# --help shows it (empty for a bare flag); the shape is part of the 1.x
# contract, so it lives here rather than in prose. A shape that opens on a
# word takes it joined too (--use=docker, --update=v1.0.0) - so --doctor's
# opens on --json, or --doctor=json would read as a target. <help> is one
# clause -
# docs/hi.1 is the long form, drift-checked against this by
# tests/hi/parse_test.sh. <needs> is what a flag wants that the payload does
# not carry: `-` for nothing (works in a session), `scripts` for scripts/ (a
# package ships it, a session does not), `git` for .git (neither a package
# nor a session has it). Rows print in this order. -h and -V are hi.sh's
# short forms of --help and --version, not rows: --help renders them itself.
--help||-|||this text
--version||-|||hi's version: the packaging stamp, or git describe
--use|<backend>|-|||skip probing and force that backend (ssh counts as one)
--plain||-|||a bare shell, nothing copied: no tar or /tmp needed
--no-plain||-|||hi's own session this once, past _HI_PLAIN=1
--keep||-|||keep the session on the target through a dropped link
--no-keep||-|||an ordinary session, past _HI_KEEP=1 or a kept one
--end||-|||close the session <target> is keeping
--preview|<subject> [--text]|scripts|_HI_PREVIEW||colors, packages, or header, as printed here
--doctor|[--json] [--problems] [--text] [--use <backend>] [ssh-options] [target]|scripts|_HI_DOCTOR||a read-only pre-flight report, or just its problems
--install|[--yes] [--link {none,user,system}] [--shell <list>] [--print-rc] [--preset <name>] [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--install|install or repair say-hi's lines in your shell rc files
--uninstall|[--purge] [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--uninstall|take those lines out; --purge removes the overlay too
--configure|[--preset <name>] [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--configure|revisit the settings, leaving the rc wiring be
--update|[<tag>] [--dry-run]|git|_HI_UPDATE||move the checkout to the newest release tag, or <tag>
--add-package|<group> <pkg>[,...] [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_ADD_PACKAGE||add rows to a group in your packages file
--remove-package|<pkg>... [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_ADD_PACKAGE|--remove|remove rows from your packages file
--add-tag|<host> <tag> [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_ADD_TAG||tag an ssh host, for the colors
--remove-tag|<host> [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_ADD_TAG|--remove|take a host's # Tags: line back out
--add-color|<type> <name> <color> [rrggbb] [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_COLOR_PIN||pin a color in your colors file
--remove-color|<type> <name> [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_COLOR_PIN|--unset|remove a pin from your colors file
--plugins|[--text]|scripts|_HI_PLUGINS_CMD||list the plugins: what rides, and what is off
--disable-plugin|<name>... [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_PLUGINS_CMD|--off|switch plugins off: nothing of theirs rides
--enable-plugin|<name>... [--dry-run]|scripts|_HI_PLUGINS_CMD|--on|switch them back on
--edit|[<file>]|scripts|_HI_EDIT||open your overlay, or one file of it, in your editor

# The words a flag or a switch takes from a fixed list, a row each:
# <flag>=<word>|<help>, the shape of the flag typed joined to its word. They
# are what `hi <flag> <TAB>` offers (common/targets.sh), and --remove-color
# takes --add-color's. Every reader of the rows above skips them: a flag
# never holds an `=`.
--link=user|~/.local/bin/hi (the default)
--link=system|/usr/bin/hi, through sudo
--link=none|no link; the wired shells alias hi either way
--shell=bash|~/.bashrc
--shell=zsh|.zshrc, under ZDOTDIR when that is set
--shell=fish|config.fish
--shell=all|every one of them installed here
--preset=everything|every feature on, and the useful package group
--preset=balanced|everything but the noise
--preset=minimal|the colored prompt and your configs, no header or editor configs
--preset=lean|minimal, and nothing of yours rides
--preset=configs|your configs along, each target's own prompt, no header
--add-color=hosttag|a # Tags: value in your ssh config
--add-color=usertag|the username on hosts carrying that tag
--add-color=username|a username
--add-color=hostname|a hostname, or a * or ? pattern
--preview=colors|every ssh host and your user, in their resolved colors
--preview=packages|the package-group legend, as the header prints it
--preview=header|the connect header, as it prints here
